this project is called the caterpillars  Cove study and Child Development Center  I think is a lot of young parents we  become incredibly sensitized to the  quality of design in children's  environments and a lot of the time the  environments that interested them the  most were ones that were a little bit  more abstract a little bit more of an  opening for them to kind of insert  themselves into it and imagine their own  environment it really was the the  concept that stayed with us throughout  the whole design process as designers I  think we bring a different part of our  design baggage and our design philosophy  to different projects so for this one  because it's for kids we wanted to give  them a lot of micro environments within  that space that they could take as just  opening suggestions for how to play one  very important part of this project  which are the little play structures and  what we try to do with those were to  create structures that were suggestive  of things like there was one which they  might think of as a factory or maybe a  dragon so that's where we had this play  space with big sand pits or sculptures  really that they could think of as hills  or islands made out of timber basically  it had the suggestions of a shape but it  wasn't complete for them and was kept  very very abstract for these kids to use  in their imaginative play we feel pretty  strongly that one of the things that's  needed to push the design scene forward  is really to foster the small and medium  size practices the young practices the  presence design award it changes the way  that people think about their  expectations for their own work it's not  just about conventionally beautiful  projects but ones that have design goals  which are not purely aesthetic our  advice to emerging young designers would  be to make sure that when you start your  office that you are ready you're  equipped to take risks take your time  and make sure that you know you're in  the right place to start your own  practice
 

